    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          The honest answer first: most healthy kids do not need protein powder at all. A child aged 4 to 8 needs about 19 g of protein a day, which is roughly two glasses of milk and an egg, and national survey data show most American children already eat well above their requirement. Where a supplement does make sense, usually for a very picky eater, a child whose growth a pediatrician is watching, or a family managing allergies or a vegan diet, the right first product is normally a kid-specific nutrition shake such as PediaSure Grow &amp; Gain, not a 25 g scoop of adult whey. Below are the protein targets by age, the situations where a shake helps, the ingredients children should never get from a tub, and eight products, kid formulas first, with the adult powders that suit older children in small portions.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="quick-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Quick Answer: Food First, Then a Kid Formula
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your child eats a reasonably varied diet that includes milk, yogurt, eggs, meat, fish, beans or tofu on most days, they almost certainly get enough protein and a powder adds nothing useful. The American Academy of Pediatrics has long said that young athletes can meet their needs from food, and that protein and performance supplements are generally unnecessary for children. That is not a cautious hedge; it is what the numbers show, and we walk through them in the next section.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where a supplement does have a place, it is usually because a pediatrician or registered dietitian has a specific worry: a toddler or school-age child who eats a handful of foods and is dropping off their growth curve, a child recovering from illness, or a family managing a milk allergy or a vegan diet who is struggling to cover the basics. In those cases the doctor is usually trying to add calories, vitamins and minerals as much as protein, which is why kid-specific nutrition shakes exist and why they are our first three picks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is how the eight picks compare at a glance. Price tiers are relative: budget means among the cheapest per serving in its category, premium among the most expensive.
          </p>

