        <div id="how-much-protein-do-kids-need" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Much Protein Do Kids Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The reference numbers come from the Dietary Reference Intakes set by the National Academies. The Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA), the amount that covers the needs of nearly all healthy children in an age group, is about 1.05 g of protein per kilogram of body weight for ages 1 to 3, and about 0.95 g/kg for ages 4 to 13. In grams per day for a reference child, that works out to the figures below.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Age</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">RDA (g/kg/day)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">RDA (g/day)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What that looks like in food</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.05</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">13 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 cup milk + 1 egg</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">4-8 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.95</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">19 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 cups milk + 1 slice cheese</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">9-13 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.95</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">34 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yogurt, a turkey sandwich and a glass of milk</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">14-18 years (girls)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.85</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">46 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">See our teens guide</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">14-18 years (boys)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.85</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">52 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">See our teens guide</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Put those numbers next to ordinary food and the gap closes fast. A cup of milk has about 8 g of protein. An egg has about 6 g. A slice of cheese has 5 to 7 g, a small pot of Greek yogurt 10 to 15 g, two tablespoons of peanut butter about 7 g, and a 3 oz portion of chicken or fish 20 g or more. A 7-year-old who has milk on cereal, a cheese sandwich at lunch and a small serving of chicken at dinner has comfortably cleared 19 g before any snacks.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why survey data from NHANES, the national nutrition survey, consistently show that most US children eat well above their protein RDA. Protein shortfalls in children in the US are uncommon and tend to cluster in specific situations: very restricted diets, food insecurity, certain medical conditions, and some poorly planned vegan diets. A child who is simply picky about vegetables is not usually short on protein; they are more often short on fiber, iron or vitamin D.
          </p>

          <p>
            Active children need a little more, but not much. Sports nutrition guidelines for adults suggest 1.2 to 2.0 g/kg, and some pediatric sports dietitians use a similar range for young athletes in heavy training, yet even at the upper end a 35 kg 10-year-old needs about 50 to 70 g a day, which an athletic appetite usually covers. If you want to sanity-check your own child, our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein needs guide</a> explains the per-kilogram math, but please treat it as a starting point for a conversation with the pediatrician rather than a prescription.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SCOOP PROBLEM</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">One adult scoop can be more than a child's whole day</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 25 g scoop of adult whey is more than the 19 g daily RDA for a 4- to 8-year-old. There is no established upper limit for protein, but a child filling up on shakes eats less of the foods that bring fiber, iron and calcium. If you use an adult powder, think in tablespoons, not scoops.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

