        <div id="when-a-supplement-makes-sense" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. When Might a Pediatrician Suggest a Protein Shake?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pediatricians do recommend supplements, just not as a routine. The most common reason is faltering growth: a child whose weight or height is crossing down through percentile lines on the growth chart. In that case the doctor is usually most concerned about total calories, and a complete nutrition shake such as PediaSure is designed for exactly that job, delivering energy, vitamins, minerals and a modest amount of protein in a drink a reluctant eater will often accept.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Picky eaters</h3>

          <p>
            Ordinary pickiness is a normal phase, especially between ages 2 and 6, and most children grow out of it while still growing normally. Extreme selectivity is different. Some children accept only a dozen or so foods, gag on new textures or have a recognized feeding disorder such as ARFID (avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder). For those children, a feeding team may use a nutrition shake as a bridge while working on expanding the diet, and in that setting the shake is one tool in a plan, not the whole plan.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Allergies, vegan diets and medical needs</h3>

          <p>
            Families managing multiple food allergies, particularly milk, egg and nut allergies together, can find protein and calcium hard to cover. A well-planned vegan diet can meet a child's needs, but it takes more attention, especially for younger children with small stomachs. Children with conditions that raise needs or limit intake, such as cystic fibrosis, inflammatory bowel disease, cerebral palsy or recovery from surgery, may be prescribed specific medical formulas. In every one of these cases the right product depends on the child, which is why a pediatrician or a registered dietitian should choose it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is not a good reason</h3>

          <p>
            Wanting a child to grow taller, bulk up for youth football or keep up with a teammate who drinks shakes are not good reasons. Protein beyond what a child needs does not make them taller, and the AAP advises against performance-enhancing substances in young athletes. Products marketed with height claims deserve particular skepticism: a child's height is determined mostly by genetics and overall nutrition, and no protein powder has been shown to add height to a child who already eats enough.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="kid-formulas-vs-adult-powders" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Kids Protein Shakes vs Adult Protein Powder: What Is the Difference?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Kid-specific shakes and adult protein powders look similar on a shelf, but they are built for opposite jobs. A kids nutrition shake is a mini-meal: modest protein, meaningful calories from carbohydrate and fat, and a broad vitamin and mineral blend. An adult protein powder is a concentrated protein source with as few other calories as possible, designed to help a grown-up hit a high protein target without extra fat or carbohydrate.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Feature</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Kids nutrition shake</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Adult protein powder</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein per serving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 6-12 g</td>
