                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical use</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gap-filler for a child not eating enough</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Extra protein for adult training goals</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The trade-off with kids shakes is sugar. PediaSure and Orgain Kids both carry roughly 8 to 9 g of sugar per serving, per their labels. That is less than a typical chocolate milk, and the calories are often the point for an underweight child, but for a child who eats well a daily sweetened shake is not a free extra. The trade-off with adult powders is dose and testing: the scoop is too big, and only a minority of brands are independently certified.
          </p>

          <p>
            A middle path that many dietitians suggest is skipping both and using food-based protein boosts instead: Greek yogurt in smoothies, milk instead of water in oatmeal, an egg whisked into pancake batter, beans blended into pasta sauce. These add protein and the other nutrients that come with it, and they teach the habit you actually want a child to keep.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-avoid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Should a Kids Protein Powder Never Contain?
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Caffeine and other stimulants</h3>

          <p>
            Some protein products, especially those sold as pre-workout or "energy" blends, include caffeine, green tea extract, guarana or other stimulants. The AAP has stated that energy drinks and stimulant-containing products should not be consumed by children and adolescents. Coffee-flavored protein powders often contain real coffee or added caffeine as well, so read the label rather than trusting the flavor name.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Creatine</h3>

          <p>
            Creatine is one of the best-studied supplements for adults, but the evidence in children is thin and the AAP recommends against performance-enhancing supplements, including creatine, for athletes under 18. Several sports blends add creatine to protein; for a child, choose a product that contains protein and nothing in that category.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sugar alcohols, proprietary blends and "fat burners"</h3>

          <p>
            Sugar alcohols such as erythritol, sorbitol and maltitol can cause bloating, gas and loose stools, and children, with their smaller bodies, often notice the effect at lower doses. Proprietary blends that list ingredients without amounts make it impossible to know what your child is actually getting. Anything marketed for weight loss, cutting or thermogenesis, including our own <a href="/best-fat-burning-protein-powder/">fat-burning protein powder</a> category for adults, is not appropriate for a child.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heavy metals</h3>

          <p>
            Independent testing groups, including Consumer Reports and the Clean Label Project, have reported measurable lead and other heavy metals in many protein powders, with plant-based and chocolate products often testing higher on average. Because children are smaller and more vulnerable to lead, contaminant screening matters more for them than for adults. Our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> explains what the test results do and do not mean, and why certifications such as NSF Certified for Sport are a useful filter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="is-protein-powder-safe-for-kids" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is Protein Powder Safe for Kids?
          </h2>

          <p>
            In small amounts, from a reputable, tested product, protein powder is not dangerous for a healthy child. Protein itself is a normal food nutrient, and a tablespoon of whey stirred into oatmeal is nutritionally similar to the milk protein in a glass of milk. The safety questions are mostly about the product, not the protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first issue is regulation. In the US, protein powders are sold as dietary supplements or foods, and the FDA does not approve them before they go on sale. A brand is responsible for its own label, and independent testing has repeatedly found products that contain less protein than claimed, undeclared ingredients or contaminants. That is the reason certification matters, and the reason we favor products listed in the NSF Certified for Sport registry for children. Our broader article on <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> covers the regulatory picture in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second issue is kidneys, which comes up often. In healthy children with normal kidney function, higher protein intake has not been shown to cause kidney damage. Children with known kidney disease, metabolic disorders such as PKU, or other medical conditions should only use protein supplements under medical supervision, and allergies to milk, soy, egg or nuts obviously rule out the products containing them.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">BEFORE YOU BUY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Three questions for the pediatrician</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Is my child growing normally on their curve? Is there a specific nutrient you are worried about? If a shake would help, which product and how much per day? The answers will tell you whether you need anything on this page at all.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How to Give a Child Protein Powder (If You Do)
          </h2>

          <p>
            Measure in grams of protein, not scoops. For a 6-year-old, an extra 5 to 10 g a day is a meaningful amount. With an adult whey at roughly 25 g per 30 g scoop, that is about a tablespoon to a heaped tablespoon, or a quarter to a third of a scoop. For a kids shake, follow the label and the doctor's instructions; these products are dosed for children already.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hide it in food the child already eats. Unflavored whey or isolate stirs into oatmeal after cooking, Greek yogurt, pancake or waffle batter, muffins, and fruit smoothies with little change in taste. Our guides to <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-smoothies/">protein powder for smoothies</a> and <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-baking/">protein powder for baking</a> have tips on texture: whey can make baked goods dry if you swap too much flour, so start by replacing no more than a quarter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for tummy trouble. Whey concentrate contains some lactose, sugar alcohols and gums can cause gas, and any new product can upset a small stomach. Start with a small amount, keep it to one new product at a time, and stop if your child has bloating, diarrhea, rash or other symptoms. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-sensitive-stomach/">sensitive-stomach guide</a> explains which formats are gentlest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="young-athletes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What About Young Athletes?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Children who swim, play soccer or run cross-country several times a week burn more energy and need a little more protein than less active peers, but their bigger appetites usually take care of it. The priorities for a young athlete are, in order: enough total food, enough carbohydrate to fuel training, fluids, and sleep. Protein comes after those, and it is rarely the limiting factor.
          </p>

          <p>
            For recovery after practice, low-fat chocolate milk is hard to beat for a child: roughly 8 g of protein per cup plus carbohydrate, fluid, calcium and vitamin D, at a fraction of the cost of a sports product. A yogurt with fruit, a turkey sandwich or a bowl of cereal with milk does the same job. These are what most sports dietitians suggest for kids before anything from a tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            Things change somewhat in the teenage years, when training loads rise, growth spurts increase needs, and some athletes enter drug-tested competition where certified products matter. That is a different set of questions, which is why we cover it separately in our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-teens/">teens guide</a>, including how much protein teenage athletes actually need and which certifications drug-tested high school and college athletes should look for.
          </p>
        </div>

