        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a powder before checking the diet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most children already eat more protein than they need. Before buying anything, add up a normal day of meals and snacks. If milk, yogurt, eggs, meat, fish or beans appear on most days, a supplement is unlikely to help, and the money is better spent on food your child enjoys.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Giving a child a full adult scoop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 25 g adult scoop is more than the entire daily RDA for a child aged 4 to 8. If an adult powder is used at all, think in tablespoons. A quarter to a third of a scoop stirred into food adds a meaningful amount without crowding out the rest of the day's eating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Picking a sports blend with extras</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pre-workout, energy and mass-gainer products often contain caffeine, creatine or other stimulants that the AAP advises children to avoid. Coffee and "energy" flavors may contain caffeine too. For a child, choose a product that contains protein and little else, and read the full label every time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting height and growth claims</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                No protein powder has been shown to make a well-fed child taller. Height depends mostly on genetics and overall nutrition. Products that promise extra inches use marketing language, not evidence. If you are worried about growth, the right first step is a growth chart review with the pediatrician.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting shakes replace meals</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A daily sweetened shake can quietly replace the dinner that would have supplied fiber, iron and vegetables. Unless a dietitian has built a shake into a feeding plan, serve it alongside food, keep it occasional and treat it as a bridge rather than a habit.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

