                  There is no official minimum age, but children under about 4 rarely have any reason to use an adult protein powder, and kids nutrition shakes typically state their own age range on the label, often 1 or 2 years and up. For any child, ask the pediatrician first. Teens have different needs, covered in our teens guide.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein does a 10-year-old need?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The RDA for ages 9 to 13 is about 0.95 g per kilogram of body weight, or roughly 34 g a day for a reference child. A cup of milk, a yogurt and a turkey sandwich nearly cover it. Very active children need somewhat more, but their larger appetites usually supply it without a supplement.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is whey protein safe for kids?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Whey is a milk protein, the same family as the protein in a glass of milk, and it is generally safe for children without a milk allergy. The concerns are dose, since an adult scoop is large for a child, and product quality. Choosing an NSF Certified for Sport product and using a small amount addresses both.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best protein powder for picky eaters?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For a picky eater whose growth a doctor is watching, a complete kids nutrition shake such as PediaSure Grow and Gain is usually the best fit, because it adds calories, vitamins and minerals as well as protein. For a picky eater who is growing normally, unflavored whey stirred into foods they already accept is a gentler option.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can protein powder hurt a child's kidneys?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  In healthy children with normal kidney function, higher protein intake has not been shown to cause kidney damage. Children with kidney disease, metabolic disorders or other medical conditions should only use protein supplements under medical supervision. The more practical risks are shakes displacing other foods and exposure to contaminants in untested products.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Should my child athlete take protein after practice?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  A snack with protein and carbohydrate after practice is a good habit, but it does not need to come from a tub. Low-fat chocolate milk, yogurt with fruit or a sandwich does the job well and costs less. If a pre-teen athlete uses a powder, choose an NSF Certified for Sport product and keep the portion small.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
